      "id": "frontier-science-120s",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "duration_s": 10.9185,
          "headline": "A multiscale question",
          "id": "question",
          "narration": "Design a fish that can glide. That request spans genes, cell programs, growing tissues, fin mechanics and whole-animal behavior.",
          "start_s": 0.0
        },
        {
          "duration_s": 22.0785,
          "headline": "Claims, assumptions and evidence",
          "id": "commons",
          "narration": "Our approach is to make each connection inspectable. MorphoSwarm Frontier brings sourced claims, equations, assumptions and evidence into a shared workspace for people and agents. Published zebrafish studies connect transporter and ion-channel variants to altered fin proportions. They give us biological hypotheses, not a demonstrated route to flight.",
          "start_s": 10.9185
        },
        {
          "duration_s": 4.4025,
          "headline": "Measured lengths constrain geometry",
          "id": "specimens",
          "narration": "We also use measured specimen lengths to constrain candidate geometry.",
          "start_s": 32.997
        },
        {
          "duration_s": 18.1185,
          "headline": "A conditional mechanical result",
          "id": "mechanics",
          "narration": "At the mechanical scale, consider a fin modeled as an end-loaded elastic beam. We formalized a stiffness condition that keeps deflection below one tenth of its length. Lean compiled the theorem; a corrupted law failed. The proof checks the implication. Measurements must establish whether the assumptions fit a real fin.",
          "start_s": 37.3995
        },
        {
          "duration_s": 10.8105,
          "headline": "Inspect the model chain",
          "id": "studio",
          "narration": "In the studio, you can inspect the equations, change design parameters and follow their evidence. We ran bending, aerodynamic, bioelectric and schooling models.",
          "start_s": 55.518
        },
        {
          "duration_s": 15.346499999999999,
          "headline": "A computational result has a boundary",
          "id": "limits",
          "narration": "Some exchange numerical outputs; genetic and developmental connections still need calibration. In a separate exploration, Evo2 generated sequence continuations. Model likelihood does not establish promoter function or cell-specific expression.",
          "start_s": 66.3285
        },
        {
          "duration_s": 15.7065,
          "headline": "Check, compare, preserve failures",
          "id": "validation",
          "narration": "Validation is a first-class part of the design: check units and assumptions, test numerical resolution, compare with observations, and preserve failures. A failed flow benchmark remains failed. An independently identified print defect becomes a revision.",
          "start_s": 81.67500000000001
        },
        {
          "duration_s": 22.618499999999987,
          "headline": "Share evidence and open questions",
          "id": "community",
          "narration": "Every unresolved bridge becomes a question or proposed experiment. Through MCP, contributors can retrieve exact claims, submit artifacts and challenge results. This is an early foundation for Lean for biology: reusable knowledge that says what was checked, where it applies, and what evidence is needed next. The flying fish is one demanding test case for that larger scientific commons.",
          "start_s": 97.38150000000002
        }
      ],
      "title": "From biological evidence to testable design"
    }
  ],
